.TH OD 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
od \- dump files as octal bytes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B od
.RB [ \-\- ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B od
writes the contents of each
.I file
to standard output as a listing of octal byte values.
If no
.I file
is given, or a
.I file
is
.BR \- ,
standard input is read.
.PP
This is a deliberately small subset of the traditional utility: only the
default format is implemented.
No output-format, address-radix, skip or count options are supported.
.PP
Each output line starts with the byte offset, in octal, padded to seven
digits, followed by up to 16 bytes, each printed as a space and a
three-digit octal number.
The offset runs on across all operands, so several files are dumped as
if they were one concatenated stream.
After the last byte a final line holding only the total byte count
(again as a seven-digit octal offset) is printed.
Unlike other implementations, repeated lines are never collapsed into
.BR * .
.PP
If the first argument begins with
.B \-
and is not
.B \-
itself or
.BR \-\- ,
.B od
reports that the option is not supported and exits with status 1
without producing any output.
Only the first argument is checked this way; later arguments are always
treated as file names.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All input was dumped.
.TP
.B 1
An option was given, a file could not be opened, or a read error
occurred.
Files that cannot be opened are reported and skipped; the remaining
operands are still dumped.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
$ printf \(aqAB\en\(aq | od
0000000 101 102 012
0000003
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cat (1),
.BR sum (1),
.BR wc (1)
.SH BUGS
None of the POSIX options
.RB ( \-A ,
.BR \-j ,
.BR \-N ,
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-v )
or the traditional format letters
.RB ( \-b ,
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-o ,
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-x )
are implemented.
